Add the display stack: a built-in IOSurface and an IOMobileFramebuffer that
drives the bootloader's framebuffer.

Ported from the OnePlus 9 port, which runs the same 1080x2400 panel and is in the
same situation: the bootloader leaves framebuffer memory in RAM that the panel
scans out by itself, so no DPU work is needed to get an image. KebabSurface
publishes that memory as an IOSurface, KebabFramebuffer (AppleCLCD, an
IOMobileFramebuffer) copies CoreAnimation's swaps into it on the CPU and drives
vsync from a 60 Hz timer.

Includes the sibling's later fixes: a repeated lookup of a surface this client
already owns reports it with -1 instead of counting a second reference, and the
console is muted with a flag rather than switched, because the console switch
writes a read-only-late variable on arm64. IOCoreSurfaceRoot gets a personality so
IOSurface.framework can connect, and the canvas dimensions are published on the
platform expert, which is the parent this framework version reads them from.

Completes the display ABI the iOS 16 backboardd calls. SwapEnd now queues the swap
record and the vsync timer presents it, so the backend is replaceable; SwapWait(6)
waits for that completion with a bounded timeout and distinguishes completed,
timed out and never queued. The selectors 12/15/17/22/27/29/33/50/55/57/68/74/79/83/84
are implemented where this port has the state to answer (power, display device,
display area, dot pitch, frame info, displayed surface) and return
kIOReturnUnsupported where honouring them would need a DPU, a backlight or a
parameter block that does not exist; unknown selectors no longer return a zeroed
success. IOSurface gains values (9/0xa/0xb) backed by a real property dictionary
and bulk attachments (0x1b) copied into the shared state page. The display is
presented as the iPhone X identity userland expects, 1125x2436 at 3x, while the
panel, canvas and default surface stay at the bootloader's 1080x2400; the CPU copy
maps logical coordinates to the panel and clips the margin.

